**1. Analysis & Description of the Attached PDF:**
The PDF contains two scientific articles by F. Lu et al., investigating Nano-11, a dendrimer-like α-D-glucan nanoparticle derived from sweet corn, as a vaccine adjuvant. The first paper (2015) introduces Nano-11's in vitro ability to bind protein antigens, be phagocytized by dendritic cells (DCs), activate them (increasing costimulatory molecules and IL-1β secretion), and enhance in vivo antibody responses with transient local inflammation. The second paper (2017) delves deeper into its in vivo kinetics, showing Nano-11 is retained at the injection site, gradually cleared, and effectively targets antigens to migratory DCs in draining lymph nodes without systemic distribution. Both conclude Nano-11 is a safe and effective adjuvant.

**2. Independent Analysis & Description of the Image:**
The image presents flow cytometry data in dot plots, illustrating antigen (OVA) uptake by myeloid cells in mouse draining lymph nodes on Day 1 and Day 2 following immunization. The top row shows Alexa Fluor 647-conjugated OVA fluorescence versus CD11c expression for three formulations: OVA alone, OVA with Aluminum Hydroxide (AH), and OVA with Nano-11 (N11). Mean Fluorescence Intensity (MFI) values for OVA are indicated. The bottom row, gated on OVA-positive cells, further differentiates cell populations based on MHCII versus CD11c expression, with percentages shown for specific cell compartments, likely representing different antigen-presenting cell subtypes.

**3. Relevance of the Image to the Scientific Topic of the PDF:**
The image is highly relevant to the strictly scientific topic of the PDF. It is Figure 5a from the second paper included in the PDF ("Alpha-D-glucan nanoparticulate adjuvant induces a transient inflammatory response at the injection site and targets antigen to migratory dendritic cells"). The flow cytometry data directly supports the paper's findings by visually demonstrating how Nano-11 (labeled "OVA+N11") significantly enhances the delivery and uptake of the model antigen (OVA) by antigen-presenting cells (indicated by CD11c and MHCII markers) in the draining lymph nodes, compared to OVA alone or with aluminum hydroxide. This directly illustrates Nano-11's mechanism as a vaccine adjuvant by targeting antigens to key immune cells.